  "answer": "Covalent compounds have low melting points compared to ionic compounds because the forces of attraction between covalent molecules are weak. In ionic compounds, there are strong electrostatic forces of attraction between oppositely charged ions, which require a lot of energy to break. This is why ionic compounds have high melting points. On the other hand, covalent compounds are usually gases or liquids at room temperature, and their molecules are held together by weak intermolecular forces. As a result, they have low melting points and low boiling points.",
